/**
 * \page reads3_page Reading Remote Data ☁️
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * AqNWB supports reading data from remote sources using different HDF5 Virtual File Drivers (VFDs).
 * Depending on the data source and your HDF5 installation, you can use the \ref reads3_ros3 "ROS3 VFD"
 * (for Amazon S3) or the \ref reads3_remfile "remfile VFD" (compatible with any HTTP(S) 
 * server supporting byte-range requests).
 *
 * \warning
 * Both remote drivers require specific build-time configurations
 * (see the \ref user_install_page "Installation" page):
 * - The **ROS3 VFD** must be enabled in your HDF5 installation. AqNWB checks this via the `H5_HAVE_ROS3_VFD` macro.
 * - The **remfile VFD** requires `libcurl` and must be enabled with the `AQNWB_USE_REMFILE` CMake option.
 *
 * \section reads3_ros3 Reading via the ROS3 VFD
 *
 * AqNWB supports reading data directly from Amazon S3 using the HDF5 ROS3 Virtual File Driver (VFD).
 * This allows you to access NWB files stored in the cloud without having to download the entire file first.
 *
 * \subsection reads3_ros3_open Opening a file from S3
 *
 * To open a file from S3, we create an \ref AQNWB::IO::HDF5::HDF5IO "HDF5IO" object with the S3 URL
 * and then call \ref AQNWB::IO::HDF5::HDF5IO::openS3 "openS3" with the AWS region.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/test_ros3_read_example.cpp example_ros3_setup
 *
 * \subsection reads3_ros3_read Reading objects and data
 *
 * Once the file is open, reading objects and data works exactly the same as reading from a local file.
 * For example, we can read the \ref AQNWB::NWB::NWBFile "NWBFile" object:
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/test_ros3_read_example.cpp example_ros3_nwbfile_read
 *
 * We can also search for objects, e.g., here to find the `TimeSeries` objects. 
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/test_ros3_read_example.cpp example_ros3_find_objects
 *
 * We can then also read data subsets as usual. Because we are using the ROS3 VFD,
 * only the metadata and the specific data chunks requested are downloaded from S3, making
 * this approach efficient for accessing specific parts of large NWB files in the cloud.
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/test_ros3_read_example.cpp example_ros3_read_data
 *
 * \section reads3_remfile Reading via the remfile VFD
 *
 * As an alternative to ROS3, AqNWB can read remote files with the
 * [remfile-cpp](https://github.com/catalystneuro/remfile-cpp) virtual file driver, a C++
 * port of the Python [remfile](https://github.com/magland/remfile) package. Instead of \ref AQNWB::IO::HDF5::HDF5IO::openS3 "openS3",
 * call \ref AQNWB::IO::HDF5::HDF5IO::openRemote "openRemote":
 *
 * \snippet tests/examples/test_remfile_read_example.cpp example_remfile_setup
 *
 * Once opened, reading from the file works exactly like in the ROS3 example above. For a complete example see
 * [tests/examples/test_remfile_read_example.cpp](https://github.com/NeurodataWithoutBorders/aqnwb/blob/main/tests/examples/test_remfile_read_example.cpp).
 *
 * Compared to ROS3, the remfile VFD:
 * - does not require HDF5 to be built with ROS3 support. It only requires libcurl and the light-weight remfile-cpp. remfile support 
 *   is enabled with the ``AQNWB_USE_REMFILE`` CMake option (OFF by default). If remfile-cpp is not installed, then AqNWB will build it directly 
 *   as part of its built process, so only libcurl is required as a separate install.
 * - works with any HTTP(S) server that supports byte-range requests, not just S3,
 *   including presigned URLs
 * - uses adaptive read-ahead caching, which coalesces sequential reads into
 *   geometrically growing range requests and is typically faster for large
 *   sequential reads
 *
 * Availability at compile time is indicated by the ``AQNWB_HAVE_REMFILE_VFD`` macro.
 *
 * \section reads3_further_reading Further reading
 * - \ref read_page discusses the general process of reading data from NWB files in more detail.
 */